/* Generated by js/buildPracticeIndex.mjs from the lessons' own drills. Do not edit by hand. */
(window.TL_DRILLS = window.TL_DRILLS || []).push.apply(window.TL_DRILLS, [{"slug":"action-base-rates","title":"Base Rates First","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>The reversal pattern fires, and a colleague says \"this is the turn\"</h3> <p>The reversal pattern from this lesson has just printed on your chart. Your history says 5% of days are turns, the pattern shows up before 60% of turns, and it also shows up on 20% of ordinary days. A colleague says it \"looks exactly like\" the last big turn and wants to bet heavily on it. The trade risks 1 to make 3. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Treat it as a 60% call. The pattern appears before 60% of turns.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the hit rate, not the probability. It leaves out the 190 false alarms in every 1,000 days. Sizing a 13.6% chance as if it were 60% is a large over-bet.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Start from 5%, multiply the odds by an LR of 3 to get about 13.6%, and check the payoff against that.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Posterior odds are 3:19. At 3 to 1 the expected value is 0.136 × 3 − 0.864 × 1, about −0.45 per unit risked. The trade is a pass at this payoff. It would need to pay more than about 6.3 to 1 to break even.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Ignore the pattern and use the 5% base rate.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safer than 60%, and here it gives the same answer: pass. But the pattern is real evidence with an LR of 3. Throwing it away would cost you on a trade with a bigger payoff.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Wait for two more momentum indicators to agree, then multiply all three LRs.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Indicators built from the same price move are one piece of evidence, not three. Multiplying their LRs double-counts it and pushes the probability up for no real reason.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative morning built on the lesson's reversal-pattern example, not a real date. Of 1,000 days, 220 show the pattern and only 30 of those are turns. At a 3-to-1 payoff the trade loses money on average, however much the chart resembles a famous turn. Writing the 5% down first is what stops the resemblance from setting the size.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-calibration-scoring","title":"Scoring Your Own Calls","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative quarterly review</div> <h3>Your next call feels like a 90%</h3> <p>You have just scored a quarter of 40 calls, the same tally as the worked example. Your ten calls written at 80% or 90% came true five times. Your twelve 60% calls came true seven times. The next morning a setup appears that feels like a 90% call, at an even payoff. How do you size it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Size it as a 90% call. This one really is different.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">At even payoff, Kelly from 90% is 2(0.9) − 1 = 80% of the bankroll. Your 90% calls have come true about half the time, so that is many times the size your record supports. Every high-confidence call felt different too.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Use the corrected number from your table, about 54%, and size it the same as your ordinary calls.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The shrunk hit rate for the 90% bucket is 53.6%, which gives a Kelly fraction of about 7%. This follows the rule the review produced: until your 80% and 90% calls beat your 60% calls, they get the same size.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Halve what the 90% would say, to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Half of 80% is 40% of the bankroll. That still sizes off the felt number. Against a measured edge near 54%, it is more than five times the Kelly fraction, deep in the zone where growth turns negative.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Skip the trade until you have more calls on record.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It cannot over-bet, but the call still looks better than a coin flip, and you only learn your calibration by making and scoring calls. Taking it at the corrected size does both.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative review built on the lesson's worked example, not a real trade. The felt 90% would size at 80% of the bankroll; the measured 53.6% sizes at about 7%. The gap between those two numbers is what an unscored confidence costs. Keep scoring: if the next 40 calls show your high-confidence calls pulling ahead, the table will let them earn more size.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-combining-signals","title":"Combining Signals That Disagree","date":"2007-08-06","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2007-08-06\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · week of 6 August 2007</div> <h3>Your \"diversified\" quant book is losing fast</h3> <p>You run a market-neutral equity book built from many signals: value, momentum, earnings quality and more. You sized it for many separate sources of return, so you run it with leverage. This week it is losing unusually fast, while the stock market as a whole has moved comparatively little. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Add to it. With so many independent signals, losses this fast must be noise.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This counts the signals instead of measuring them. If the losses are coming all at once across \"different\" signals, that is evidence they are not independent right now. Adding doubles down on one crowded position.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Measure how correlated your positions are right now, not in calm times. Treat the book as the number of bets it really is, and cut risk to fit.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the effective-number idea applied to positions. Correlations measured in calm periods can understate how much a set of different-looking bets is one bet. Sizing to the honest count keeps you in the game whatever happens next.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hold and wait. Each signal still has a long record.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The records may be real, but they do not tell you the book is diversified this week. Holding leveraged risk that is really one position is a bet on surviving the path, not a decision about it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch off the signals that are losing most and keep the rest.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">If the losses come from a shared crowded position, the signals you keep are likely in the same trade. Cutting by recent pain rather than by measured overlap leaves the real exposure in place.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In the week beginning 6 August 2007 a number of large quantitative equity funds suffered unusually large losses within a few days. On 7 and 8 August they were hit hard while equity and bond markets moved relatively little; only on 9 August did the S&amp;P 500 fall, by nearly 3%. Amir Khandani and Andrew Lo later explained that many funds had arrived at similar portfolios from similar signals. When one or more large portfolios were unwound quickly, the selling pushed prices against everyone holding the same positions, forcing further selling. A sharp partial rebound followed on Friday 10 August. The diversification had been counted, not measured.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/conference/2007/liquidity/Khandani_Lo.pdf\">Khandani and Lo, \"What Happened to the Quants in August 2007?\", 2007 (Federal Reserve Bank of New York conference copy)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/papers/w14465\">Khandani and Lo, NBER Working Paper 14465, November 2008</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-edge-after-costs","title":"Does the Edge Survive Costs?","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research review</div> <h3>A \"highly significant\" fade lands on your desk</h3> <p>A colleague brings you an intraday EUR/USD fade: 3,000 trades over three years out of sample, about 1,000 a year, average gross profit 0.9 pips, standard deviation 15 pips. The t-statistic is 3.29. All-in cost per round trip, measured honestly, is 1.3 pips. They want to start trading it on Monday. What do you say?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Go ahead. A t of 3.29 means the edge is real.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Real, yes, and smaller than its cost. At 0.9 pips gross and 1.3 pips a round trip, every trade loses 0.4 pips on average, about 400 pips a year. More trades make the loss more certain, not smaller.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Not as it is. Its break-even cost is 0.9 pips, under the 1.3 it pays. Rework it to trade less on bigger moves, fix any filter in advance, and test it again on fresh data.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the cost-check card: compare the edge per trade with the full round-trip cost, then look for a version with room to spare. A filter chosen after seeing the results has to be confirmed on data it was not chosen on.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Trade it, but with a cheaper broker to cut commission.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cutting cost is the right instinct, but even free commission leaves 0.1 pips a trade, a net t near 0.37, and a loss if costs double. It is marginal at best, not a strategy.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it at double the size to make the small edge worth the effort.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Size multiplies the net edge, and the net edge is negative. Bigger size also tends to mean worse fills, which raises the cost further.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This drill is illustrative: it uses the lesson's worked example, not a real fund. The same pattern is documented in the real world. Perold's 1988 paper showed Value Line's real fund earning far less than its paper portfolio, and Novy-Marx and Velikov (2016) found that many high-turnover stock anomalies lost most or all of their premium to realistic trading costs.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-edge-decay","title":"When an Edge Has Stopped Working","date":"2007-08-06","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2007-08-06\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · the week of 6 August 2007</div> <h3>Your quant strategy has its worst few days ever, and the market barely moved</h3> <p>You run a quantitative long/short equity strategy with a long tested record. In the space of a few days this week it has suffered sudden, severe losses, far outside anything in its history. The broad stock market has hardly moved. Other quant funds, running different models, are reporting the same thing. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The edge is dead. Liquidate everything today.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A few days of losses is the most vivid part of the record and the least informative about the edge. When many funds hold the same positions, selling into the unwind means selling near the worst prices.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut to a size you can survive under a pre-set rule, change nothing in the model, and judge decay separately on the long record.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This keeps the two questions apart. The sudden loss looks like a crowding shock, which calls for survival, not a verdict. Whether the edge itself has been eroding is a slower question that only the long record, tested against the tested edge, can answer.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hold full size. It is only noise and it will come back.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It might come back, but holding full size through a crowded unwind assumes you will survive the path, and nothing guarantees it. It also skips the slower question of whether the edge has been shrinking.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-fit the model on this week's data so it stops losing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is in-sample optimisation on the worst sample you have. It fits the shock, not the edge, and resets nothing.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In the second week of August 2007, many quant long/short equity funds took sudden, severe losses in the same few days. They were hit hardest on 7 and 8 August, while equity and bond markets moved relatively little, and the losses continued on the 9th when the S&amp;P 500 fell nearly 3%. Much of the loss reversed on Friday 10 August, but some funds had already cut positions near the worst point. Khandani and Lo later explained the losses as a crowded unwind: many funds held similar positions, and the rapid selling of one or more large portfolios pushed prices against all of them. They also found a slower decay: their stand-in strategy's returns had been falling for years as more capital chased similar trades. The week was a shock, not the decay; the decay was the quiet trend underneath.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/conference/2007/liquidity/Khandani_Lo.pdf\">Khandani and Lo, \"What Happened to the Quants in August 2007?\", 2007 (Federal Reserve Bank of New York conference copy)</a>; <a href=\"https://www.nber.org/papers/w14465\">Khandani and Lo, NBER Working Paper 14465, November 2008</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-expected-value","title":"Thinking in Expected Value","date":"2018-01-31","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2018-01-31\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 31 January 2018</div> <h3>A short-volatility note that almost never loses</h3> <p>Through the very calm markets of 2017 you have held an exchange-traded note that rises when the VIX falls. Most days, and most months, it has made a little money. The equity curve is smooth and the hit rate is high. A friend says you should double the position, since it \"basically never loses\". What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Double it. A hit rate that high is proof of an edge.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A hit rate on its own says nothing. EV also needs the size of the losing side, and with a short-volatility note the rare loss can be a large multiple of a typical win. A long run of small wins is exactly what this shape looks like before the loss arrives.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Write out the EV with the loss side made explicit: how big the rare loss could be and how often it might come. Stress both, then size so that loss is survivable.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's method. With a high win rate and a huge, rare loss, the sign of EV depends almost entirely on the loss you have not seen yet. If you cannot pin that number down, size as if it is bad.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep the position as it is. It has worked, so there is no reason to change.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than doubling, but it still judges the trade by its recent results. The question is whether the position survives the rare loss, and you have not asked it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Judge it by its smooth equity curve: low volatility means low risk.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A smooth curve is what many small wins look like. It tells you the win rate, not the tail. The risk sits in the one outcome the curve has not shown you.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On 5 February 2018 U.S. stocks fell sharply and the VIX more than doubled in a single session. The best-known short-volatility note, Credit Suisse's XIV, lost more than 90% of its value in a day, and Credit Suisse announced it would terminate it. Several other short-volatility funds took heavy losses in the same days. The win rate had been real; the payoff on the losing side was the number nobody had measured well.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">Cboe VIX daily close via FRED (17.31 on 2 February, 37.32 on 5 February 2018)</a>; Credit Suisse media release, \"Credit Suisse AG Announces Event Acceleration of its XIV ETNs\", 6 February 2018.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-trade-plan","title":"From a View to a Trade Plan","date":"2015-01-14","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2015-01-14\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 14 January 2015</div> <h3>Long EUR/CHF just above the 1.20 floor</h3> <p>Since September 2011 the Swiss National Bank has held a minimum rate of 1.20 francs per euro, promising unlimited purchases to defend it. For more than three years EUR/CHF has sat just above 1.20. You are long EUR/CHF near the floor with a stop a little below it. Your analysis says the floor is the invalidation level: below 1.20, the thesis is dead. How do you size the trade?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Size it to the short stop as usual. The stop is close, so a big position only risks a small amount.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This assumes the stop fills near its level. A stop is an order, not a guarantee. If the floor goes, there may be no prices between 1.20 and wherever the market lands, and a big position turns a small planned loss into a huge one.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep the floor as invalidation, but add a gap line: the loss if price jumps far past the stop. Size so that loss is one you can survive.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The invalidation is right; the exit assumption is the weak point. When a thesis rests on a policy or a peg, the plan sizes for the gap, not for the stop. That usually means a much smaller position.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Skip the trade. A peg can break at any moment.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe, and sometimes right. But the thesis may be sound. The problem is size, and a small position with a written gap line lets you take the trade without betting the account on the stop.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Remove the stop. The central bank has promised unlimited purchases, so it cannot break.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This turns an analysis into a belief. A plan with no exit for being wrong has no maximum loss at all.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> On the morning of 15 January 2015 the SNB abandoned the floor without warning. The franc jumped in minutes and EUR/CHF briefly traded well below parity. Stops placed just under 1.20 were filled far lower, wherever the next real price was. Losses were many times what plans had allowed, some retail clients ended with negative balances, the UK arm of the broker Alpari went into insolvency, and FXCM needed an emergency rescue loan.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.bis.org/review/r150116a.htm\">Thomas Jordan, SNB press conference remarks on discontinuing the minimum exchange rate, 15 January 2015 (BIS reprint)</a>; Finance Magnates, \"Leucadia Hands FXCM $300 Mln Life-Line\", 16 January 2015.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"action-vol-to-size","title":"From a Volatility Forecast to a Position Size","date":"2018-01-26","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2018-01-26\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · late January 2018</div> <h3>Your volatility-targeted sleeve keeps getting bigger</h3> <p>You run a stock-index sleeve at a 10% volatility target, sized from trailing realised volatility. 2017 was one of the calmest years for US stocks on record, and the VIX spent much of it at historically low levels. Your trailing forecast has fallen so far that the rule now asks for about 2× leverage, the most the sleeve has ever held. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Follow the rule to 2×. The forecast says risk is low, so the position is only taking its normal 10% risk.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The forecast only knows the past. A position sized from trailing calm is largest exactly when a shock would hurt most, and the forecast learns about the shock only after it arrives.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Apply a leverage cap and a floor under the forecast, and check it against implied volatility before letting the calm number set the size.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">These are the three fixes the lesson names. The cap limits the damage if calm ends suddenly, the floor stops the forecast assuming volatility will stay unusually low, and implied volatility looks forward where trailing realised looks back.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop volatility sizing and hold a fixed notional instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids the calm-market trap, but throws away the useful part: when volatility does jump, a fixed position takes far more risk than planned. Volatility sizing is right; it just needs a cap and a floor.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Also cut the risk budget whenever volatility rises, to be extra safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is double scaling: size then falls with the square of volatility. It does nothing about the real problem, which is the oversized position while volatility is still low.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> In late January and early February 2018 stocks fell. On Monday 5 February the VIX more than doubled in a single session, its largest one-day percentage rise on record. Credit Suisse's XIV, the biggest inverse-VIX note, lost almost all its value, and Credit Suisse moved to terminate it. Many post-mortems also pointed to selling by strategies that size by recent volatility, which had to cut exposure after the jump; how much each group contributed is still debated.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS\">Cboe VIX daily close via FRED (17.31 on 2 February, 37.32 on 5 February 2018, a 116% rise; 2017's average close was 11.1)</a>; Credit Suisse media release, \"Credit Suisse AG Announces Event Acceleration of its XIV ETNs\", 6 February 2018.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-apt","title":"Arbitrage Pricing Theory","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative CPI morning</div> <h3>Inflation is high, and it is exactly what everyone expected</h3> <p>Your book has a large negative exposure to inflation surprises: it loses when inflation comes in above forecast. This morning's inflation print is the highest in years, but it lands exactly on the consensus forecast, and futures had priced that number for weeks. A colleague says the book is \"obviously wrong-footed by high inflation\" and wants to cut the position before lunch. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Cut it. Inflation this high has to hurt a book with negative inflation exposure.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That reads the level, not the surprise. The factor in the model is inflation minus what was expected, and today that is zero. Nothing new arrived for the book's exposure to price, so trading on the headline number pays costs for no change in risk.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Leave the position. The surprise was zero, so this print adds no new risk to that exposure. Review the exposure itself if you think the next surprise is more likely to be upside.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the Chen-Roll-Ross logic. Only the unexpected part carries a premium or moves the price. If you are worried, the honest question is about the odds of a future upside miss, which is a decision about the exposure, not a reaction to today's on-consensus number.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Halve it, to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cheaper than cutting all of it, but it still answers a headline rather than a surprise. If you wanted less inflation-surprise exposure, that was true yesterday too. Halving now just pays costs for the feeling of doing something.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Double it. High inflation must mean it falls back from here.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is a forecast of the level dressed up as a factor view. APT says nothing about where inflation goes next. It prices exposure to surprises, and doubling the exposure doubles your bet on the next miss, whichever way it goes.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the framework says.</strong> This morning is illustrative, not a specific historical release. The point is the lesson's Section 03: an anticipated number, however extreme, is already in the price, so a factor built from the level would credit or blame the book for news nobody received. Measure the surprise first; only then ask whether the exposure is the one you want.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-avellaneda-stoikov","title":"Avellaneda-Stoikov Inventory-Aware Market Making","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative afternoon on the desk</div> <h3>Hit on the bid all afternoon, now long</h3> <p>You have been quoting a currency pair symmetrically around the mid. For the last hour a steady wave of sellers has been hitting your bid, and you are now long 5 units, the lesson's worked example: mid 100.00, risk aversion 0.1, volatility 2, half the session left. Your quotes are still 99.25 bid and 100.75 offer. What do you change?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. The spread is fair, and the flow will even out.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Symmetric quotes have no brake. If the selling continues, every fill adds to the position, and nothing in the quote discourages it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-centre both quotes on the reservation price, 99.00: bid about 98.25, offer about 99.75.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the model's answer. The offer moves closer to the mid, so buyers take inventory off you; the bid moves away, so you stop adding. The width stays 1.4908, and the skew fades as you get back to flat.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Widen both quotes symmetrically around the mid until the flow stops.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Wider quotes around the mid make it harder to sell as well as to buy, so the position just sits there. In this model inventory moves the centre of the window, never its width.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Pull the bid entirely and keep the offer where it is.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It stops the position growing, like a hard position cap, but it is blunt: you stop earning on one side, and you do nothing to make the offer more attractive. The reservation price does the same job continuously.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's worked example, not a real trading day. Skewing both quotes against your inventory is the model's whole mechanism. One caveat it does not cover: if that one-sided flow was informed, being long is worse than just risky, and the spread also needs an adverse-selection charge on top.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-barra-risk-models","title":"Barra-Style Risk Models","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday risk report</div> <h3>The stock picker who is mostly running a momentum bet</h3> <p>You run an equity book sold to clients as bottom-up stock picking. This morning's factor risk report says 84.6% of your active variance comes from factor exposures, mostly a large momentum tilt you never set out to take, and only 15.4% from your individual stock picks. Your best ideas this year happen to be recent winners. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. Factor exposure is just a label the vendor puts on your stocks; your picks are what matter.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The label is attached to real co-movement. If momentum reverses, most of your book falls together whatever each company's story is. Ignoring the report means your clients are paying for stock picking and getting a factor bet.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Keep your picks but use the risk model as an optimiser constraint: cap the momentum tilt, offset it where you can, and re-run until most of the active risk is stock-specific.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the model is for. It shows where the risk comes from and lets you shape it. After the change, your results will mostly reflect your stock selection, which is the bet you meant to make.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Lean in. Momentum has been working, so add more high-momentum names.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That uses a risk model as an alpha model. Everyone with the subscription sees the same momentum scores, so they are not an edge. You would be adding to an unintended bet because it has paid lately.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cut every position by a third to bring total risk down.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Total risk falls, but the mix does not change: still about 85% factor and 15% specific. You are smaller, not different. It is a defensible stopgap while you fix the tilt properly.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the method says.</strong> This Monday is illustrative; the 84.6% split is the lesson's own worked example scaled up to a book. The lesson's point is that a commercial risk model is a measuring and constraining tool. Its job is to tell you which bets you are really running so you can keep the ones you mean and shed the ones you don't.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-bates-model","title":"Bates Model: Stochastic Volatility + Jumps","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative week before a data release</div> <h3>Your Heston fit says the weekly wings are far too expensive</h3> <p>You run an FX options book. Heston, calibrated to your 3-month and 1-year implied vols, fits them tightly. A scheduled release falls inside next week's expiry, and your model prices the one-week out-of-the-money options well below where the market trades them. The model's smile at one week is close to flat; the market's is steep. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell the one-week wings. The model says they are overpriced.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Heston's smooth diffusion cannot build a steep skew in a week, so its \"fair value\" for the wings is low by construction. You would be selling jump risk at a price set by a model with no jumps in it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Refit Heston's five parameters to the one-week slice alone.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That may patch the one-week price, but the parameters then stop fitting the 3-month and 1-year slices you already had right. This is the slice-by-slice trap the lesson warns about.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Add a jump term and calibrate all maturities jointly, then treat the short-dated wing as jump risk that delta-hedging cannot remove.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Jumps steepen the short end without bending the long end, because their kurtosis fades like one over maturity. And a jump cannot be rebalanced through, so the wing deserves its own risk limit, not just a vega number.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Keep Heston and add a hand-set vol spread to short-dated wings.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A pragmatic patch that gets prices closer, but it has no mechanism behind it, so it says nothing about how that risk behaves or how to hedge it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative case based on the lesson's own trading scenario, not a real episode. With the lesson's fixed jump settings, excess kurtosis from jumps is 7.744 at one week against 0.149 at one year, 52 times larger, while jumps keep the same 38.6% share of variance at both. One honest caveat stays: a better joint fit does not prove the split between jump risk and volatility risk is the true one, and the hedge depends on that split.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-bipower-variation","title":"Bipower Variation","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative payrolls Friday</div> <h3>One minute made the whole day look volatile</h3> <p>You forecast next week's volatility from daily realized variance. Friday was quiet except for one minute: the payrolls release moved the pair 46 bp in a single one-minute bar, with ordinary 1 bp wiggles either side. Friday's realized variance came out over 100 times what the same session would have shown without that minute, and 99% of it was the one minute. Bipower variation for Friday is only about 6% of its realized variance. What goes into your forecast?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Friday's full realized variance. A big number is a big number, and volatility clusters.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That treats a one-off jump as if the whole day had been turbulent. Jump variance is far less persistent than continuous variance, so feeding it in at full weight inflates next week's forecast and shrinks your positions for a regime that never started.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Split it: use the continuous part (bipower variation) as the persistent input, and treat the jump part (RV − BV) separately, with much less carry-over.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the split is for. Andersen, Bollerslev and Diebold found the jump component far less persistent than the continuous one, so a forecast that weighs them differently reflects how volatility actually behaves after a news spike.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Delete Friday from the sample as an outlier.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Closer to right than using the full number, but it throws away the continuous variance Friday did have, and it pretends jumps never happen. The next scheduled release will jump too; dropping days hides a risk instead of measuring it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The jump was up, so lean long next week.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Bipower variation measures how much of the day was jumps, not which way the next move goes. The split is about magnitude. Nothing in it is a directional signal.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the method says.</strong> This Friday is illustrative: the numbers are the lesson's 15-minute worked example (one 46 bp jump among 1 bp moves), not a specific dated release. The decision rests on the lesson's sourced point: realized variance mixes two kinds of risk, and bipower variation is how you separate the part that tends to persist from the part that tends to fade.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-breeden-litzenberger-variance-swaps","title":"Breeden-Litzenberger &amp; Variance Swaps","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>The skew steepened overnight, but at-the-money vol barely moved</h3> <p>You trade index options. Overnight, out-of-the-money puts got noticeably more expensive relative to out-of-the-money calls, while the at-the-money implied vol is almost unchanged. Your head of desk asks what the market is now pricing, and what you would do about it.</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Vol is up, so buy at-the-money straddles.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">At-the-money vol barely moved. The repricing happened in the wing, so a straddle bets on the part of the distribution that did not change.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Smooth the surface, extract the density before and after, and see where the probability moved before choosing a tail trade.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's own use case. The density shows the left tail thickening while the body near spot stays put: a specific statement about where the market's fear concentrated, which tells you which strikes to trade.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Check the variance-strip fair vol, VIX-style, and report that it rose.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">True and model-free, but one number. It says total priced variance went up, not where in the distribution the change sits.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Take butterflies straight from the raw quotes and report any negative density as a crash signal.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Differencing raw quotes twice amplifies noise. A negative density is a data-quality flag, never a probability.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own trading scenario, not a dated episode. A Breeden-Litzenberger extraction run before and after turns \"the skew steepened\" into a picture: more risk-neutral probability on a sharp fall, the centre of the distribution largely unchanged. That is more specific than \"vol went up\", and it needs a smoothed surface, because the second derivative amplifies every wiggle in the quotes.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-capm","title":"The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative allocation meeting</div> <h3>A manager with four points of alpha</h3> <p>A manager's fund looks like the lesson's Asset B: over five years its beta to the market was 1.50 and it averaged 13.5%, against a market average of 7% and a risk-free rate of 2%. CAPM predicts 9.5%, so the pitch is \"four points of alpha a year\". The fund holds mostly small, cheap-looking companies. Do you pay for the alpha?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Yes. Four points above the Security Market Line is skill.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A single-factor alpha cannot separate skill from pay for a risk CAPM leaves out, or from luck in five annual observations. A tilt to small and value stocks is exactly the kind of exposure that showed up as \"CAPM alpha\" in the data.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-measure the alpha after adding size and value factors, and ask for a much longer record before calling it skill.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's own warning, and the next lesson's tool. If most of the four points disappears once size and value are accounted for, you would be paying active fees for a factor tilt you could buy cheaply.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hedge out the beta with index futures and keep the rest.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Sensible portfolio engineering: beta is the right hedge ratio. But hedging the market does not answer whether the remaining return is skill, a size and value tilt, or noise.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Reject it. CAPM's assumptions are false, so the alpha means nothing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">False assumptions make CAPM a baseline, not a useless one. The alpha is a real measurement against that baseline; the question is what explains it, not whether to ignore it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's toy Asset B, not a real fund. The real-world counterpart is well documented: Fama and French (1992) found that small and value stocks earned more than their betas predicted, so a small-value tilt shows up as positive CAPM alpha. That is why alpha is now judged against multi-factor models, not beta alone.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-covar","title":"CoVaR: Conditional Value-at-Risk for Systemic Risk","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative risk meeting</div> <h3>Two EM currencies, the same VaR, and dollar funding starting to tighten</h3> <p>You hold two emerging-market currencies, X and Y, of equal size. Measured on their own, their 5% VaRs are almost identical. A tail regression on a dollar funding-stress indicator gives X a slope of 0.8, the worked example's line, so its ΔCoVaR against funding stress is −2.4%. Y's slope is close to zero, and so is its ΔCoVaR. Funding conditions are starting to tighten and you have to cut risk in one place. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. The two VaRs haven't moved, so the risk hasn't changed.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Standalone VaR is computed on each currency alone, so it cannot see the spillover. The risk that grows when funding tightens is exactly the part your VaR report leaves out.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cut both by the same amount.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It lowers risk, but it spends half the cut on Y, whose tail barely responds to funding stress. You give up Y's return for little protection.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Cut X first. Its tail is the one that worsens when funding is in distress.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">X's ΔCoVaR says its bad-day return drops about 2.4 extra points when funding stress hits its own VaR. Y's barely moves. Cutting X removes the co-moving exposure that standalone VaR cannot show.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Cut Y. Its losses come from its own country, which feels harder to predict.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That keeps the position whose tail is tied to the shock you are worried about. Y's idiosyncratic risk is already in its VaR; X's extra spillover is not.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's toy line, not a real episode. If funding stress reaches its own 5% level, X's conditional 5% return falls from −0.4% to −2.8%, while Y's stays near its baseline. Two caveats from the lesson still apply: a tail slope fitted on few points is noisy, and a large ΔCoVaR shows co-movement, not proof that funding stress causes X's losses.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-dupire-local-vol","title":"Dupire's Local Volatility Model","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative client request</div> <h3>A down-and-out barrier on a pair whose smile you have already marked</h3> <p>You run an FX exotics book with a full, liquid USD/JPY vanilla smile marked at one expiry, as in the lesson's scenario. A client asks for a price on a down-and-out barrier option on the same pair and expiry. Its value depends heavily on the path spot takes towards the barrier. How do you price it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use the at-the-money implied vol for the whole thing.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">One flat vol is inconsistent with your own marked smile. A client could hedge the barrier with your vanillas and trade against the mispricing.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Build local vol straight from the raw vanilla quotes with finite differences.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The strike curvature amplifies noise. In the lesson's own numbers, a 5-cent change in one quote moves local vol from about 20.06% to 22.59%. Unsmoothed quotes can even give negative local variance.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Fit a smooth surface, build local vol from it, price the barrier, and stop there.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The price is now consistent with every vanilla on the book. But a barrier is sensitive to how the smile moves, and local vol's forward smile is typically too flat, so this inherits that weakness unexamined.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Fit a smooth surface, price with local vol for vanilla consistency, and check the price against a stochastic-vol or SLV model before quoting.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Local vol gives the exact match to today's vanillas. A model with tested forward dynamics shows how much the barrier depends on the smile's future path, which is the part local vol gets least right.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own trading scenario, not a real trade. The point: local vol reprices every vanilla exactly by construction, which removes the static-hedge inconsistency, but its implied forward smile is widely considered too flat. For a path-dependent exotic like a barrier, that is exactly the risk to check, and it is why larger desks often use stochastic-local-vol hybrids for barriers.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-factor-marginal-component-var","title":"Factor VaR, Marginal VaR &amp; Component VaR","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative morning</div> <h3>VaR jumped 14% overnight, and nobody traded</h3> <p>You run the lesson's three-position book: A long $100,000, B long $150,000, C short $50,000. Overnight, total 95% VaR rose from $6,272 to $7,177, about 14%. No position changed size and no volatility estimate moved. The new breakdown shows A's slice up from $2,567 to $3,092, B's up from $2,621 to $3,139, and C's down from $1,084 to $947. The risk committee wants an answer and an action. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing changed in the book, so the VaR figure must be a data error. Ignore it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Sizes and volatilities are only part of the input. The covariance matrix also holds correlations, and a shift there moves VaR with no trade at all.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Read the breakdown as a correlation shift between A and B, which now move together more, and trim or hedge that pair.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A and B both grew while their sizes and volatilities stood still, and C's slice shrank. That pattern points at the A and B correlation, so the fix is targeted. Trimming A and B by a fifth each, keeping C, brings VaR to about $5,949.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Cut every position by 14% to get back to yesterday's number.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It restores the headline, to about $6,173, but it cuts C too, whose slice actually fell. Across-the-board de-risking spends effort where the risk did not come from.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Close C. Shorts are the risky part of any book.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">C was not the cause. Closing it leaves VaR at about $6,634, still above yesterday, and removes a position that partly hedges A.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative case on the lesson's worked-example book, not a real episode: the jump comes from raising the A and B correlation from 0.30 to 0.80 and recomputing every number with the lesson's formulas. It shows the lesson's own point. A component breakdown separates \"one position got riskier\" from \"positions started moving together\", which a single VaR number cannot do.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-fama-french-carhart","title":"Fama-French &amp; Carhart: Beyond the Single Factor","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative FX fund pitch</div> <h3>\"Currency alpha\" that might just be carry</h3> <p>An FX manager shows several years of steady returns that a regression on the broad dollar alone cannot explain, and calls the residual alpha. The book is usually long high-yielding currencies and short low-yielding funding currencies, with some trend-following on top. The fee is set like a stock-picker's. What do you do before allocating?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Allocate. The returns are steady, and the dollar factor does not explain them.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is the CAPM mistake in currency form. With only one factor in the regression, any systematic carry or momentum tilt has nowhere to go except alpha.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Regress the returns on the dollar, carry and FX momentum factors, and see how much alpha survives.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's method transplanted, and the published currency factors exist for exactly this. If carry and momentum soak up most of the return, you are being asked to pay an active fee for factor premia you could buy far more cheaply.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Allocate, but only to the part of the book that is not carry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The right instinct, but you cannot know which part that is without the factor regression. The trend-following piece may itself be ordinary FX momentum.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Treat the carry return as free money and ask the manager to lever it up.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A factor premium is pay for a risk. Carry is short a crash: when yen-funded positions unwound in 2024, AUD/JPY fell about 17% from 11 July to 5 August. Leverage turns that kind of month into years of lost carry.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What this teaches.</strong> This is an illustrative pitch, not a real fund. Its anchors are documented: a published FX carry factor (Lustig and Verdelhan, 2007) and FX momentum factor (Menkhoff, Sarno, Schmeling and Schrimpf, 2012) exist, and carry's crash risk showed up in the 2024 AUD/JPY unwind. The lesson's rule is the same in any market: take out the known factors first, then judge what is left.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-fama-macbeth","title":"Fama-MacBeth Two-Stage Regression","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research meeting</div> <h3>A carry signal with a t-statistic of 4</h3> <p>A colleague tests a carry signal on 25 currency pairs over 120 months. She stacks all 3,000 pair-months into one regression of next month's return on the signal and reports a t-statistic of 4.0. You rerun it Fama-MacBeth style: one cross-sectional regression per month, then the average slope and its time-series standard error. That gives t = 1.4. The team wants to know whether to put capital behind the signal. What do you recommend?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Go with the pooled result. Three thousand rows is a big sample, and t = 4 is far past 2.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Those rows are not independent. In any month a dollar shock moves most of the 25 pairs together, so the pooled regression counts each month's story about 25 times and its standard error comes out far too small. The 120 monthly slopes are the honest sample.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Report t = 1.4 as the headline, add the Shanken and autocorrelation corrections (which can only widen it), and treat the signal as unproven until a longer or fresh sample says otherwise.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the procedure doing its job. The monthly slopes disagree too much for their average to be told apart from zero, and both corrections push the same way. Saying \"not shown yet\" costs nothing; sizing up a noise premium costs money.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Add 25 more pairs to the universe and rerun, to get the t-statistic up.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">More pairs make each month's slope a little less noisy, so it can help at the margin. But the shared monthly shock does not average away across pairs that all react to it. The error bar is still driven by how many separate months you have, and widening the search after seeing the result edges toward data mining.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Drop the few months where carry crashed, since they look like outliers, and rerun.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Those months are exactly the risk the premium is meant to pay for. Deleting them shrinks the spread of the monthly slopes and inflates t by construction. That is choosing the answer, not measuring it.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the method says.</strong> This meeting is illustrative, with made-up numbers, but the gap it shows is the one the lesson is about: a pooled regression treats correlated pair-months as independent votes and overstates significance, while Fama-MacBeth counts each month once. A t of 1.4 on 120 monthly slopes is not evidence of a priced signal, and the Shanken and serial-correlation corrections only make the case weaker.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-garman-klass-yang-zhang","title":"Garman-Klass &amp; Yang-Zhang Volatility Estimators","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative Monday after a weekend headline</div> <h3>Setting a stop after a weekend gap</h3> <p>You hold a EURUSD swing position and size its stop as a multiple of 30-day volatility. The week was quiet and all your estimators agreed. Then a Friday-evening headline moved rate expectations, and EURUSD opened Monday well above Friday's close. Before Monday's bar is even finished, you need to reset the stop. Which volatility figure do you size it from?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">30-day Garman-Klass. It is about 7.4 times as efficient as close-to-close, so it is the most precise number you have.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Efficient, but blind to exactly what just happened. Its formula only looks inside each bar, so the weekend gap sits in its blind spot. It barely moves, and your stop ends up sized for a quieter market than the one you are in.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">30-day Yang-Zhang, which has a dedicated term for the close-to-open gap.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Its overnight term measures precisely the move Garman-Klass and Parkinson cannot see, and its range component is not fooled by drift. It reacts to the gap straight away, which is what a stop that has to survive the new market needs.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">30-day close-to-close volatility.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Once Monday closes, the close-to-close return will include the gap, so it is not blind to it. But it uses one price a day and is far noisier for the same window, so it reacts slowly and imprecisely. Defensible, not best.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">30-day Parkinson, since a big day will show up in the range.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Parkinson looks only at each bar's own high and low. A gap between Friday's close and Monday's open is outside both bars' ranges, so Parkinson cannot see it either.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the method says.</strong> This Monday is illustrative, the lesson's own trading scenario rather than a specific dated event. The lesson's eight-bar example makes the same point in numbers: with a single 38-pip gap in the window, Garman-Klass read 3.81% and Yang-Zhang 4.27%. When your market can gap, size risk off the estimator that measures the gap.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-glosten-milgrom","title":"Glosten-Milgrom: Sequential Bayesian Market Making","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative rate-decision hour</div> <h3>Thirty minutes to a central bank decision</h3> <p>You make prices in a major FX pair. A central bank rate decision is due in thirty minutes, and the outcome is genuinely uncertain. Over recent weeks, flow arriving just before these decisions has tended to be right more often than usual. Your normal spread is tight. What do you do with your quote through the decision?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Keep the normal spread. Volume is high and you want the business.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The informed share is higher than usual right now, so the break-even spread is wider. Quoting tight means the traders with a view pick you off, and the noise flow does not pay for it.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Widen ahead of the decision, then tighten step by step as the first trades after the release reveal where the price is going.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the model's prediction: a higher perceived informed share before the news, then one-sided post-release trades that settle your belief fast and let the spread compress, often within minutes.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Pull your quotes completely until an hour after the decision.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids being picked off, but you lose the flow and you skip the post-release window, where trades resolve the uncertainty quickly and a tighter quote is again break-even.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Widen before the release and keep the wide spread all afternoon to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Once the early trades have revealed the news, your belief is far from 50/50 and the break-even spread is narrower. Staying wide prices you out of business that is no longer dangerous.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a dated historical episode. The lesson's model and its real-world scenario point the same way: spreads widen ahead of scheduled information events because more of the flow is informed, and compress afterwards as one-sided trades resolve the uncertainty, which the lesson notes is a documented empirical pattern. Real spreads also carry inventory and processing costs, so the model explains one component, not all of it.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-granger-causality","title":"Granger Causality &amp; Lead-Lag Analysis","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research morning</div> <h3>A cross-correlation peak at lag two</h3> <p>You trade two commodity currencies against the dollar. A colleague shows you daily returns where one pair's move correlates with the other pair's move two days later, a clear peak at lag +2. He wants to trade it from tomorrow: buy the follower when the leader rises. What do you do first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trade it. A clear peak away from lag zero is a lead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A raw cross-correlation does not control for the follower's own momentum. If the follower is simply autocorrelated and the two pairs are correlated in general, you get the same peak with no extra information in the leader at all.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Run the Granger test on the two price levels, trying lag orders until one is significant.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Two mistakes at once. Trending levels produce spurious significance, and searching lag orders for the smallest p-value is data mining. Either one can manufacture a result from nothing.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Pick the lag order with AIC or BIC on returns, run the Granger F-test in both directions, and check the timestamps and closing times behind each series.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the test the lesson builds. It credits the leader only if its past helps after the follower's own past is in the model, it allows for feedback either way, and the timestamp check rules out a lead that is just one close being older than the other.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Run the Granger test one way, leader to follower, on returns at a lag picked in advance.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The core test is right. But it skips the reverse direction and the timing check, so a feedback loop or a stale-close artefact could still pass as a clean one-way lead.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a real trading day or a real result. A cross-correlation peak is a reason to run the Granger test, not a reason to trade. Even a clean significant result only says the leader's past helps forecast; a shared risk driver or mismatched timestamps can produce it with no real link between the two pairs.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-har-rv","title":"HAR-RV &amp; Realized Volatility Forecasting","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative calm day after a shock</div> <h3>Today looks calm. Do you cut your volatility forecast?</h3> <p>You set position sizes from a one-day-ahead volatility forecast. A volatility shock hit the market about two trading weeks ago and has faded: today's realized volatility is back to 0.63% a day, about 10.0% a year, near the pre-shock level. Using the lesson's 22 days, your HAR-RV forecast for tomorrow says 10.86%. A colleague wants to size off today's 10.0% instead, since \"the shock is over\". What do you size off?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Today's 10.0%. The most recent day is the best guide to tomorrow.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is a forecast with one day of memory. Realized volatility decays slowly after a shock, and one calm day right after a cluster is exactly where a copy-today forecast is most likely to be fooled. You would size up just before volatility drifts back.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">The HAR-RV forecast of 10.86%, which still weighs the past week and month.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what the model is built for. The weekly (0.4387) and monthly (0.5962) averages still carry the shock, so the forecast stays above today's reading. It encodes the long memory that real volatility shows, with weights fitted by a plain regression.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">The 22-day average alone, about 12.3% a year, to be safe.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Cautious, and it remembers the shock, but it gives today and this week no more say than a day three weeks ago. It will keep you undersized long after the market has settled. Safer than copying today, less accurate than the blend.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the method says.</strong> This day is illustrative, built on the lesson's own 22-day example and its illustrative Corsi-style weights, not a dated market event. The point is the worked example's: a forecast that only looks at yesterday can be fooled by one calm day after a cluster, while HAR-RV's weekly and monthly terms keep that memory alive. The flip side, from the pitfalls, is that the same averaging makes it slow to react when a fresh spike starts.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-hasbrouck-information-share","title":"Hasbrouck Information Share &amp; Price Discovery","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research note</div> <h3>\"Spot leads futures, with a 57% information share\"</h3> <p>A colleague's draft note says spot EUR/USD leads the futures contract, with an information share of 57%. You check the code: the Cholesky ordering put spot first, and only that one ordering was run. The two venues' VECM shocks are clearly correlated. The note goes to the head of trading this afternoon. What do you ask for?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. 57% is above half, so spot leads.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With correlated shocks, the venue ordered first claims the whole shared part of the variance. A 57% share from that ordering alone could fall to 11% with the other ordering, as in the lesson's worked example. The number reflects the ordering as much as the market.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Re-run with futures first and report that number instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Swapping one arbitrary ordering for the other is the same mistake facing the other way. Neither ordering is right, and choosing the one that tells the story you prefer is worse.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Report both orderings as a range with the midpoint, and add a measure that does not depend on ordering, such as the component share.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is Hasbrouck's own advice plus the standard literature response. If the range is wide, the honest conclusion is that this sample cannot say which venue leads, and the note should say so.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Drop the information share and compare the two adjustment speeds from the VECM instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids the ordering problem, but it answers a different question: who gets pulled back harder, not where news shows up first. Useful as a cross-check, not a replacement.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a real study or a real result for EUR/USD. Whenever the venues' shocks are correlated, a single-ordering information share is half an answer. The range between the two orderings is the result; its width tells you how much the data can really say.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-hill-estimator","title":"The Hill Estimator","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative screening run</div> <h3>One currency comes back with a tail index of 1.8</h3> <p>You screen a watchlist with the Hill estimator, as in the lesson's ranking table. A calm major pair comes back near 4.5, a typical G10 cross near 3.0, and a crisis-prone EM currency near 1.8, at the one value of \\(k\\) you happened to pick. All three are currently risked with a normal-distribution VaR. What do you do next?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use 1.8 as the EM currency's risk number and cut the position to match.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Hill gives a tail exponent, not a loss level. There is no closed-form VaR at a chosen confidence from \\(\\hat\\alpha\\) alone, so there is nothing to size against yet.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Recompute it across a range of k and sub-samples, then build a full GPD fit for the EM currency first.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the screening use the lesson describes. One estimate at one \\(k\\) can swing a long way, so check that 1.8 is stable, then spend the expensive fitting effort on the name that looks fattest.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Fit a full GPD to all three names straight away.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Not wrong, and you get loss levels for everything. But it skips the point of a cheap screen, and the thin-tailed major may not need the effort.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Declare the EM currency's variance infinite and drop variance-based risk for it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Below 2 is the variance-breaking zone, but one Hill number is not proof. On the lesson's simulated sample with a true index of 3, the estimate ran from about 4.25 down to 1.73 depending on \\(k\\).</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson's numbers say.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own illustrative ranking table, not a real screening result. The live Hill plot shows why the second option wins: on 300 simulated draws whose true tail index is 3, \\(\\hat\\alpha\\) moves from 4.25 at \\(k = 6\\) to 2.18 at \\(k = 7\\) and wanders near 2 after that. A single reading, whatever its value, is a prompt to look harder, not a risk number.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-jump-detection-tests","title":"Lee-Mykland &amp; Boudt-Croux-Laurent: Testing for Jumps","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative busy open</div> <h3>Your jump detector fires at the open, again</h3> <p>You clean jumps out of your realized-volatility input before forecasting. Your detector runs plain Lee-Mykland on 5-minute bars with a full-session critical value of 5.40 and a yardstick that averages the whole session at about 10.2 bp. This morning it flagged a 60 bp move in the opening bar (L = 5.88), as it does most mornings. The open's typical move is 18 bp. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Trust the flag: strip the bar from the volatility input as a jump.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The flag comes from the clock, not from a jump. Against the open's own typical 18 bp the move is about 3.3, nowhere near 5.40. Stripping ordinary open bars every morning removes real volatility and biases your forecast low.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Add the time-of-day correction: divide each return by its bucket's typical size before testing, then re-run.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the Boudt-Croux-Laurent fix. The open bar scores 60 ÷ 18 ≈ 3.33 and is cleared, while quiet-period moves get judged against their own small baseline. It fixes the false alarms at the open and the misses at midday at the same time.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Raise the critical value to 6 for the whole day.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It stops this one false alarm, but it makes the midday problem worse: a real midday jump already needs about 55 bp to clear the plain test, and now it needs more. You have traded false positives for more false negatives instead of fixing the yardstick.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to 1-minute bars for finer resolution and keep the same settings.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The window, the number of tests and the critical value all belong to one bar size. On 1-minute bars microstructure noise adds false jumps, and the old threshold no longer has the false-alarm rate you chose.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the method says.</strong> This morning is illustrative, using the numbers from the lesson's own calculator (60 bp at the open, plain L 5.88 against 5.40, corrected L 3.33). The lesson's point is that a plain test run against the intraday U-shape errs both ways, and the cure is to deseasonalise before testing, not to move the threshold.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-kyle-obizhaeva-invariance","title":"Kyle-Obizhaeva: Market Microstructure Invariance","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative first trade</div> <h3>Your first order in a thin small-cap</h3> <p>Your desk trades the lesson's mega-cap A every day and has a cost model calibrated on years of its fills. Today it must buy 2,000 shares of small-cap B for the first time: $20, 200,000 shares a day, 45% annual volatility. There is no execution history in B at all. The order is 1% of B's daily volume. How do you set your expected cost?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use A's cost per 1% of volume. It is the same share of the day's volume, so it should cost the same.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Same share of volume is not the same risk. B's daily volatility is 2.835% against A's 1.575%, so even the square-root law alone puts B at about 28.3 bps against 15.7 bps for A: nearly twice the cost.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Compute B's price, volume and volatility, rescale the model calibrated on liquid names to B's activity, and treat the result as a starting estimate to check against your first fills.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is what invariance is for: carrying a calibrated shape to a name with no history, using only the three inputs of W. It is a principled starting point, not a guarantee, so you watch the first fills and adjust.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Trade a few tiny test orders over the next weeks and build B's own cost curve before doing the real size.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Safe, but slow, and tiny tests tell you little about the cost of a real-sized bet. Invariance gives you a reasonable estimate on day one without that delay.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Skip the estimate. B is a different market, so nothing from A applies.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is exactly the \"every market is unique\" view the lesson argues against. Measured in risk and scaled by activity, the evidence from US equities is that the two markets share far more than their raw numbers suggest.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's two example stocks, not a real order. A thin, volatile name costs more at the same share of volume, and invariance is how a desk turns a liquid-name model into a day-one estimate for it. Keep the caveats: it is a regularity with scatter, and most of its evidence comes from equities.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-ledoit-wolf-shrinkage","title":"Ledoit-Wolf Covariance Shrinkage","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative rebalance</div> <h3>Forty series, two years of data, and an optimiser that wants extreme positions</h3> <p>You are building a diversified overlay across 40 FX pairs and macro factors from two years of daily returns, about 500 days, as in the lesson's scenario. Fed the raw sample covariance matrix, the mean-variance optimiser returns very large long and short weights, and they change sharply when you shift the data window by a month. What do you change first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. The sample covariance is unbiased, so the weights are the honest answer.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Unbiased is not the same as accurate. With 40 series and 500 days the smallest eigenvalues are biased low, and inverting the matrix turns that noise into oversized positions.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Replace the matrix with a scaled identity: equal variances, no correlations.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">That is shrinkage with the dial at 1 toward the most aggressive target. It throws away every real correlation in the basket, which is all bias and no data.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Shrink the covariance toward a structured target with the Ledoit-Wolf intensity, and handle expected returns separately, for example with Black-Litterman.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Shrinkage fixes the covariance side: a better-conditioned matrix and weights less sensitive to the window. The expected-return side is a separate source of the same symptom and needs its own fix.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Apply Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage and treat the problem as solved.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A real improvement, and the right first step on the risk side. But noisy expected returns can still drive extreme weights on their own.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative case built on the lesson's own scenario, not a real episode. With \\(q = 40/500 = 0.08\\), the sample matrix sits in the regime where Random Matrix Theory predicts real eigenvalue distortion. Shrinkage pulls the smallest eigenvalues up and the largest down, which is why the shrunk matrix inverts more calmly. It still cannot fix a structural break inside the two-year window.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-market-depth-slope","title":"Market Depth, Order-Book Slope &amp; Depth Imbalance","date":"2014-10-15","html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\" data-date=\"2014-10-15\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · 15 October 2014</div> <h3>A large Treasury futures order on a morning when the book is thin</h3> <p>It is the morning of 15 October 2014. Treasuries have been rallying, and when you look past the best bid and offer in the 10-year futures book, the size resting within a few ticks is far below what you normally see: cumulative depth barely grows beyond the touch. You have a large sell order to complete before noon. How do you work it?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Send the whole order as one market order now and be done with it.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">With little resting beyond the touch, a large market order walks through level after level, and each one is further away. A shallow slope is exactly the warning that size will move the price fast.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Read cumulative depth a few levels deep, cut child orders well below what is resting there, and work them patiently.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This sizes each slice to the liquidity that is actually there rather than to the quote. On a thin day, smaller slices and more time keep each one from walking the book.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">The quoted spread is still one tick, so size the slices as you would on a normal day.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The quote shows only the front door. Depth and slope are what tell you the cost of size, and both are saying this is not a normal day.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Hold the whole order until depth recovers, then trade normally.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It avoids walking a thin book, but you carry the full position and its price risk while you wait, and you still have a deadline. Patient slicing gets some of it done safely.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What happened.</strong> Between about 9:33 and 9:45 a.m. New York time, the 10-year Treasury yield fell roughly 16 basis points and then came back, with no clear news to explain it. The US agencies' joint staff report on the event documented how thin order book depth was around the move. A large order sent into that book would have paid heavily for walking it.</p><p class=\"tl-drill-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/news/aboutthefed/2015/oa150713.html\">Federal Reserve Bank of New York, release of the Joint Staff Report on October 15, 2014, 13 Jul 2015</a>; <a href=\"https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2019/10/from-the-vault-a-look-back-at-the-october-15-2014-flash-rally\">Liberty Street Economics, \"A look back at the October 15, 2014 flash rally\", 15 Oct 2019</a>.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-obizhaeva-wang-impact","title":"Obizhaeva-Wang Transient Price Impact","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative execution hour</div> <h3>Laying out a large sell order over the next hour</h3> <p>You must sell a large EUR/USD order within the hour, in five child orders at evenly spaced times. Your estimate is that the book heals at a finite, moderate rate: as in the lesson's toy example, about 40% of one child order's push has healed by the time the next one goes. Using the lesson's toy costs as a guide (one block 50.00, even split 26.45), how do you lay out the five child orders?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Sell it all in the first child order and be done.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">One block pays the full self-impact of the whole size at once, with no time for the book to heal: 50.00 in the toy example, almost twice the even split.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Make the first and last child orders larger and trade lighter in between.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">At the start the book is fully rested, and whatever the last order leaves behind never hurts a later trade. The middle orders push into your own leftover impact, so they should be smaller. In the toy example, 40 / 6.67 / 6.67 / 6.67 / 40 costs 25.92, the cheapest of the schedules shown.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Two half-size blocks, at the start and the end, with nothing in between.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Pushing the idea to its extreme backfires. Each block pays so much self-impact that it swamps the healing saved: 28.38 in the toy example, worse than an even split.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Five equal child orders.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Close to the best: 26.45 against 25.92 in the toy example, about 2% more. A sensible default when you do not trust your estimate of how fast the book heals.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This is an illustrative situation using the lesson's toy numbers, not a real order or a real calibration. With finite resilience the cheap places to trade bigger are the two ends. The saving over an even split is modest, and it depends on a healing rate that is hard to estimate and moves through the day, so the even split remains a respectable fallback.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-order-flow-imbalance","title":"Order Flow Imbalance &amp; the Microprice","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative quoting decision</div> <h3>The book tilts under your quotes</h3> <p>You make prices in a currency pair and re-mark your quotes off the midpoint. Over two book updates, bid size at the touch has grown from 400 to 900 while the ask has thinned from 400 to 300. Neither touch price has moved. QI is 0.5, OFI has been +200 then +400, and the microprice sits a quarter pip above the midpoint. What do you do with your quotes?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Nothing. The midpoint hasn't moved, so fair value hasn't either.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The midpoint ignores size. With the ask this thin, an informed buyer can lift it and move the price up, and your stale offer is the one that gets taken just before the move.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Re-centre your bid and ask on the microprice, leaning both up a little, and keep updating on every book event.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This is the lesson's desk use: OFI as the early warning, the microprice as the reference you quote around. You avoid being picked off on the thin side and keep earning the spread.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Pull your offer completely until the book rebalances.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It protects you from the thin-ask move, but you stop making a two-sided market and lose flow. Leaning the quote does the same job more cheaply.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Skew your quotes far above the market and hold them there for the next hour.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">These signals speak to the next few ticks, not the next hour. The 900 on the bid can also be cancelled an instant later; a spoofed wall would leave you badly mispriced.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's own four-snapshot book, not a historical episode. In that example, the very next update moved both touch prices up a pip, with OFI +800, as the tilt had suggested. The lesson's cautions still apply: displayed size is not committed size, and the signal is short-horizon and top-of-book only.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-pin","title":"The Probability of Informed Trading (PIN)","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research screen</div> <h3>The stock at the top of the PIN screen</h3> <p>A research screen ranks stocks by PIN fitted on a quarter of daily buy and sell counts. Top of the list is a thinly traded small cap at about 0.31, roughly double a typical name in your universe. Its fitted \\(\\delta\\) is high, so most of its information events look like bad news. A colleague wants to treat it as a stock full of informed sellers and short it. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Short it. High PIN and high δ together mean informed money is selling.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">PIN is direction-free and says nothing about which way the next move goes. A fitted δ from one quarter is a weak basis for a trade, and the whole reading may not be information at all.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Treat it as a flag to investigate: check the trade classification behind the counts, refit from several starting points to rule out a local optimum, and test whether ordinary illiquidity-driven imbalance explains the number.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Each of these is a documented weak point: classification errors flow straight into PIN, the likelihood has local optima and boundary solutions, and Duarte and Young argue part of PIN is symmetric imbalance in illiquid names rather than private information.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Switch to VPIN instead, since it is newer and updates in real time.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">VPIN answers a related question faster, but it is a rougher approximation of the same idea, not a strictly better replacement, and it does not resolve whether the imbalance is information.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Ignore the screen. PIN is contested, so it is useless.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Contested is not the same as empty. PIN is a well-defined, widely used measure of information risk; the right response to the dispute is to check what drives a given reading, not to throw the measure away.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical episode. The lesson's points decide it: PIN measures how much of the flow is informed, never which way; it is only as good as the classification and the numerical fit behind it; and Duarte and Young (2009) argue a real share of it reflects illiquidity-driven imbalance. A high reading in a thin stock is a question to investigate, not a trade.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-reality-check-spa","title":"White's Reality Check &amp; Hansen's SPA Test","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative research review</div> <h3>Your best variant passed, but only five were tested</h3> <p>You swept 50 variants of a strategy over the same history. Forty-five looked poor, so you dropped them. Of the five you kept, the best averages 0.47% a period, and a Reality Check run on those five gives \\(p = 0.003\\). A colleague wants to put capital behind it this week. What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Approve it. A p-value of 0.003 is far below any threshold.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The test answered \"is the best of these five real?\" The search was fifty wide. Dropping the forty-five losers first means the replay never saw how many chances luck really had, so the small p-value says nothing about the search you actually ran.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Rerun the test, SPA ideally, on all fifty variants including the dropped ones, then check the survivor on data the search never touched.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The test is only honest on the full candidate set. SPA keeps the forty-five weak variants from padding the null, so you lose little power by including them. Passing it is still not proof: a separate out-of-sample check comes after.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Skip the bootstrap and run the Deflated Sharpe Ratio with fifty trials instead.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Better than the five-candidate test, because it counts the real search. But it uses a count and an estimated spread of Sharpes in a bell-curve shortcut, when you already hold all fifty return histories and can bootstrap them directly.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Switch to whichever of the five has the highest Sharpe, since Sharpe already adjusts for risk.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A high Sharpe can come from luckily small swings rather than a real mean edge. In this lesson's panel, Carry's Sharpe of 0.93 sat close to the real edge's 1.19, yet its mean failed the bootstrap test with \\(p \\approx 0.24\\).</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical episode. The rule that decides it is the lesson's own: a Reality Check or SPA test is only as honest as the candidate set you feed it. A p-value from five hand-picked survivors of a fifty-variant search is worthless as evidence, and no later step can detect that. Rerun on all fifty, and even then treat a pass as \"not just luck on this sample\", with an out-of-sample check still to come.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-roll-spread-estimator","title":"Roll's Implicit Spread Estimator","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative backfill</div> <h3>One month comes back with no spread</h3> <p>You are backfilling a monthly spread history for a thin FX cross from trade prints, because no usable quote feed goes back that far. Most months give a sensible Roll estimate. One volatile, trending month comes back with a positive covariance, so Roll returns nothing. The backtest needs a number for every month. What goes in that month's cell?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Zero. No estimate means no measurable spread.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">A positive covariance means the model's assumptions failed that month, usually a trend or correlated order flow. It says nothing about the spread being small, and a volatile month is when spreads are usually wider, not narrower.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Copy the previous month's estimate forward quietly.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It hides the failure. Anyone reading the series later sees a smooth, populated history and has no idea that month's number was never measured.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Record it as missing with a flag, cross-check with a high-low estimator such as Corwin and Schultz, and charge the backtest a cost you state openly for that month.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This keeps the gap visible and uses an estimator that does not need a negative covariance. Whatever cost the backtest uses there is then a labelled assumption, not a fake measurement.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Widen the window to two months until the covariance turns negative.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">You may get a number, but it averages the troubled month with a calmer one and assumes the spread stayed constant across both. Defensible as a rough fill if you say so, but it blurs exactly the month you care about.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative situation, not a historical episode. The lesson's guidance is direct: an undefined Roll window is a normal outcome in short or trending samples, and it must be represented explicitly as missing, never as zero and never silently interpolated. A high-low estimator such as Corwin and Schultz (2012) is the natural cross-check because it does not share Roll's failure mode.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-trade-classification","title":"Trade Classification: Lee-Ready &amp; Bulk Volume Classification","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative data decision</div> <h3>Signing EUR/USD spot flow with no consolidated quote tape</h3> <p>You want buy and sell volume for a VPIN-style toxicity signal on EUR/USD spot. Your fills come from several liquidity providers. You have trade prices and volumes from all of them, and timestamped quotes from just one provider, whose timestamps are sometimes out of order. Which classifier do you build on?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Lee-Ready, with the five-second lag, against that one provider's quotes.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Lee-Ready needs the prevailing best bid and ask for the whole market at every trade, correctly timed. One provider's book is not the market in a fragmented spot market, the timestamps are unreliable, and a five-second lag is far too coarse for a fast market.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Bulk Volume Classification on volume bars, with σ estimated from recent history only.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It needs only price and volume, which you have for every venue. This is why VPIN was built on it. Keep in mind that it signs bars, not individual trades.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">The tick rule on the combined trade prints.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It also needs only prices, so it runs on your data. But unchanged prices just copy the last sign, so on a busy tape many calls carry no fresh information. Inside Lee-Ready it is only the tiebreaker.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Skip the signing and feed raw, unsigned volume into the signal.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Toxicity and imbalance are about the gap between buying and selling. Unsigned volume rises on both, so it cannot tell one-sided pressure from busy two-way trading.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative decision, not a historical episode. The lesson's guidance is that the classifier follows the data you really have. Lee-Ready fits consolidated, well-timed exchange quote data like TAQ. Fragmented FX and OTC markets lack that tape, which is why a price-and-volume method like BVC is the natural fit there. Where a venue's feed carries an aggressor flag, as CME futures data does, no guess is needed at all.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-triple-barrier-labeling","title":"Triple-Barrier Labelling &amp; Meta-Labelling","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative labelling decision</div> <h3>Which label do you train on?</h3> <p>You are building training labels for a model that will trade EUR/USD with a stop 3σ below entry, a target 4σ above and a 10-bar time limit. A colleague suggests labelling every entry by the sign of the 10-bar return because it is quicker. Another suggests triple-barrier labels with σ taken from the whole sample's volatility, \"because it is more accurate\". What do you do?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use the sign of the 10-bar return. Over many entries the errors should wash out.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The errors are not random. Every path that hits the stop and then recovers is labelled a win, so the model learns to like exactly the entries your stop turns into losses.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Use triple-barrier labels with σ from the full sample.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The barrier walk is fine, but a full-sample σ knows how volatile the future turned out to be. That leaks information from after the entry into where the barriers sit today.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Use triple-barrier labels with the trade's real multiples, 4σ, 3σ and 10 bars, and a σ computed only from data before each entry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The label then describes the trade you will actually run, and nothing in it peeks past the entry. This is the lesson's recipe.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Use triple-barrier labels with fixed 20-pip and 15-pip barriers for every entry.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">It respects the first-touch race, which is the main fix. But fixed widths are easy to hit on wild days and hard on quiet ones, so labels from different regimes stop being comparable.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>What the lesson says.</strong> This is an illustrative decision, not a historical episode. The lesson's rule settles it: label each entry by whichever barrier its real exit rules would hit first, with barrier widths scaled by a volatility estimate known at the entry. In the lesson's own 20-bar path, a 10-pip stop is hit at bar 5 while the 10-bar return is 9 pips up, so the quick label and the real trade disagree.</p></div> </div>"},{"slug":"institutional-vecm","title":"Vector Error Correction Models (VECM)","date":null,"html":"<div class=\"tl-drill\"> <div class=\"tl-drill-label\">Scenario drill · an illustrative pair trade</div> <h3>You can only fill one leg first</h3> <p>You trade AUD/USD against NZD/USD with a hedge ratio of 1.10. The spread has stretched to 50 pips: AUD is rich against NZD, so the trade is sell AUD/USD and buy 1.10 times as much NZD/USD. Your fitted error-correction model gives \\(\\alpha_Y = -0.35\\) for AUD and \\(\\alpha_X = +0.15\\) for NZD, both checked as significant on a pair that passed a cointegration test. Liquidity is thin and you can only work one leg now; the other has to wait a period. Which leg do you fill first?</p> <ol class=\"tl-drill-options\"> <li data-verdict=\"good\">Sell AUD/USD first, then buy the NZD/USD leg.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">AUD is the follower. The model forecasts it to fall about 17.5 pips next period, so waiting on that leg gives away the most. NZD is forecast to rise only about 7.5 pips, so delaying it costs less than half as much.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy NZD/USD first, because the anchor is the safe leg to get done.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">The anchor is exactly the leg you can afford to leave. It is expected to move least. Doing it first leaves the fast-moving AUD leg open while it does most of the correcting without you.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"bad\">Buy AUD/USD, since its large adjustment speed shows it has momentum.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">This reads the sign backwards. A negative \\(\\alpha_Y\\) with a positive spread means AUD is forecast to fall, back towards NZD. Buying it bets on the gap widening, which is the opposite of error correction.</div></li> <li data-verdict=\"ok\">Wait until both legs can be filled together.<div class=\"tl-drill-why\">Clean execution, no legging risk. But the forecast says most of the gap closes through AUD next period, so waiting may leave little of the spread to capture.</div></li> </ol> <div class=\"tl-drill-outcome\"><p><strong>The point.</strong> This is an illustrative situation built on the lesson's toy numbers, not a real fit or a real trading day. A plain z-score rule says only that the spread is wide. The error-correction model adds which leg is expected to do the moving, and that is what tells you which leg to protect first. On real data, check that the gap between the two speeds survives its standard errors before acting on it.</p></div> </div>"}]);
Object.assign(window.TL_LESSONS = window.TL_LESSONS || {}, {"action-base-rates":{"t":"Base Rates First","q":6},"action-calibration-scoring":{"t":"Scoring Your Own Calls","q":6},"action-combining-signals":{"t":"Combining Signals That Disagree","q":6},"action-edge-after-costs":{"t":"Does the Edge Survive Costs?","q":6},"action-edge-decay":{"t":"When an Edge Has Stopped Working","q":6},"action-expected-value":{"t":"Thinking in Expected Value","q":6},"action-trade-plan":{"t":"From a View to a Trade Plan","q":6},"action-vol-to-size":{"t":"From a Volatility Forecast to a Position Size","q":6},"institutional-apt":{"t":"Arbitrage Pricing Theory","q":6},"institutional-avellaneda-stoikov":{"t":"Avellaneda-Stoikov Inventory-Aware Market Making","q":6},"institutional-barra-risk-models":{"t":"Barra-Style Risk Models","q":6},"institutional-bates-model":{"t":"Bates Model: Stochastic Volatility + Jumps","q":6},"institutional-bipower-variation":{"t":"Bipower Variation","q":6},"institutional-breeden-litzenberger-variance-swaps":{"t":"Breeden-Litzenberger &amp; Variance Swaps","q":6},"institutional-capm":{"t":"The Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)","q":6},"institutional-covar":{"t":"CoVaR: Conditional Value-at-Risk for Systemic Risk","q":6},"institutional-dupire-local-vol":{"t":"Dupire's Local Volatility Model","q":6},"institutional-factor-marginal-component-var":{"t":"Factor VaR, Marginal VaR &amp; Component VaR","q":6},"institutional-fama-french-carhart":{"t":"Fama-French &amp; Carhart: Beyond the Single Factor","q":6},"institutional-fama-macbeth":{"t":"Fama-MacBeth Two-Stage Regression","q":6},"institutional-garman-klass-yang-zhang":{"t":"Garman-Klass &amp; Yang-Zhang Volatility Estimators","q":6},"institutional-glosten-milgrom":{"t":"Glosten-Milgrom: Sequential Bayesian Market Making","q":6},"institutional-granger-causality":{"t":"Granger Causality &amp; Lead-Lag Analysis","q":6},"institutional-har-rv":{"t":"HAR-RV &amp; Realized Volatility Forecasting","q":6},"institutional-hasbrouck-information-share":{"t":"Hasbrouck Information Share &amp; Price Discovery","q":6},"institutional-hill-estimator":{"t":"The Hill Estimator","q":6},"institutional-jump-detection-tests":{"t":"Lee-Mykland &amp; Boudt-Croux-Laurent: Testing for Jumps","q":6},"institutional-kyle-obizhaeva-invariance":{"t":"Kyle-Obizhaeva: Market Microstructure Invariance","q":6},"institutional-ledoit-wolf-shrinkage":{"t":"Ledoit-Wolf Covariance Shrinkage","q":6},"institutional-market-depth-slope":{"t":"Market Depth, Order-Book Slope &amp; Depth Imbalance","q":6},"institutional-obizhaeva-wang-impact":{"t":"Obizhaeva-Wang Transient Price Impact","q":6},"institutional-order-flow-imbalance":{"t":"Order Flow Imbalance &amp; the Microprice","q":6},"institutional-pin":{"t":"The Probability of Informed Trading (PIN)","q":6},"institutional-reality-check-spa":{"t":"White's Reality Check &amp; Hansen's SPA Test","q":6},"institutional-roll-spread-estimator":{"t":"Roll's Implicit Spread Estimator","q":6},"institutional-trade-classification":{"t":"Trade Classification: Lee-Ready &amp; Bulk Volume Classification","q":6},"institutional-triple-barrier-labeling":{"t":"Triple-Barrier Labelling &amp; Meta-Labelling","q":6},"institutional-vecm":{"t":"Vector Error Correction Models (VECM)","q":6}});
